# theatre-os: minimal sway config to host moonlight-qt as a single
# fullscreen wayland client, with latency tuning.
#
# Location: this file ships in the OS image at /etc/sway/config.
# Sway's config search order is ~/.sway/config, ~/.config/sway/config,
# /etc/sway/config, /etc/xdg/sway/config; with no per-user override
# (and there isn't one — /home/kodi is a bind-mounted persist subvol
# that ships empty), sway falls back here. Editing this file requires
# a new image (the iteration loop); for live experiments, drop a copy
# into /home/kodi/.config/sway/config in experiment mode and it'll
# take precedence.
#
# Why sway (not cage, not EGLFS): see README → Kodi & moonlight
# session. Short version: EGLFS makes Qt own the display and forces
# every video frame through a GL composite (+~12-17ms). Sway lets
# moonlight's VAAPI dmabufs go to a KMS plane via wlroots, with two
# additional knobs (allow_tearing, max_render_time) that EGLFS and
# cage don't expose. Measured 2-6ms render-incl-vsync on this
# hardware vs 12-17ms on EGLFS.
#
# Cage was the obvious "minimal kiosk compositor" choice, but it
# swallows stderr (no journal output) and its smaller userbase
# means fewer eyes on the wlroots-moonlight code path. Sway has
# real logging, the same wlroots underneath, and the gaming-on-
# wayland community has battle-tested it.

# Take whatever output is connected; let moonlight pick the mode.
# adaptive_sync off: AVR/projector chain doesn't speak VRR cleanly.
# max_render_time 2: wake compositor 2ms before vsync to composite
#   and hit the *current* vsync instead of the next one. Saves
#   ~one frame of latency on the compositor side. Conservative; if
#   we miss the deadline (jitter) we'd see frame drops. 2ms is
#   plenty for compositing a single fullscreen surface on an
#   Intel iGPU.
output * adaptive_sync off max_render_time 2

# Every window: fullscreen, no border, allow tearing.
# allow_tearing: submits buffers immediately on present instead of
#   waiting for vblank. The wayland equivalent of "V-Sync off" but
#   per-window and enforced by the compositor (which is the layer
#   that actually decides when the page flip happens). For moonlight
#   this means decoded frames go to scanout the moment they're
#   ready. We can't see tearing in practice — first-person content
#   on a 200" projector with moderate camera motion hides it.
for_window [shell=".*"] fullscreen enable, border none, allow_tearing enable

# Recovery escape hatch in case moonlight wedges and a keyboard is
# attached: Mod4+Shift+q kills sway, which exits this unit, which
# brings kodi-gbm back via ExecStopPost on the unit.
set $mod Mod4
bindsym $mod+Shift+q exec swaymsg exit

# Launch moonlight at startup. When it exits (clean quit, SIGINT,
# crash), exit sway too — that makes the systemd unit go inactive
# and kodi-gbm starts back up automatically.
#
# /usr/bin/moonlight is moonlight-qt (Arch's package name vs binary
# name mismatch). The unit sets QT_QPA_PLATFORM=wayland so Qt talks
# to sway's wayland socket instead of falling back to EGLFS.
exec /usr/bin/moonlight && swaymsg exit
